(typeof module==="object"&&module.exports?require("../curriculum.js"):BodyguardCurriculum).register(16,[["q16-8574d48356a3","s16.0-404f42c65aaa","16.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nclinical assessment tool that can be used to quickly evaluate neurological function and determine if specific parts of the nervous system have been affected by damage or disease","neurological exam",["stroke","conduction aphasia","receptive aphasia"],"neurological exam: clinical assessment tool that can be used to quickly evaluate neurological function and determine if specific parts of the nervous system have been affected by damage or disease.","The answer begins with N and has 2 words.","fig-ch16_00_01","f-777e8c654623"],["q16-b3ef67e6a9f0","s16.0-fa2a4fd4647f","16.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\n(also, cerebrovascular accident (CVA)) loss of neurological function caused by an interruption of blood flow to a region of the central nervous system","stroke",["neurological exam","paresis","spinocerebellum"],"stroke: (also, cerebrovascular accident (CVA)) loss of neurological function caused by an interruption of blood flow to a region of the central nervous system.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1971460","f-732e4c0f0904"],["q16-1ce86a720503","s16.0-fa2a4fd4647f","16.0","Introduction","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe problem is finding where in the entire nervous system the ____ has occurred.","stroke",["neurological exam","praxis","vestibulocerebellum"],"The problem is finding where in the entire nervous system the stroke has occurred.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1971460","f-bc54af145ce5"],["q16-2281f095e4fb","s16.0-ed8de17a53e4","16.0","Introduction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ and gray matter are not types of nervous tissue, but indications of different specializations within the nervous tissue.","White matter",["neurological exam","stroke","superficial reflex"],"White matter and gray matter are not types of nervous tissue, but indications of different specializations within the nervous tissue.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2609723","f-bb85526d2acd"],["q16-ef91363838a9","s16.0-233a415a9a74","16.0","Introduction","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ plays a very different role than the visual cortex.","hypothalamus",["stroke","neurological exam","gait"],"For example, the hypothalamus plays a very different role than the visual cortex.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2402967","f-c26090536f0b"],["q16-95aac0db1c98","s16.1-d807d3ef18b1","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmajor section of the neurological exam that assesses complex, coordinated motor functions of the cerebellum and associated motor pathways","coordination exam",["sensory exam","hemorrhagic stroke","gait exam"],"coordination exam: major section of the neurological exam that assesses complex, coordinated motor functions of the cerebellum and associated motor pathways.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1641247","f-4851e0d19afb"],["q16-5861792f9bfa","s16.1-410666712157","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmajor section of the neurological exam that assesses sensory and motor functions of the cranial nerves and their associated central and peripheral structures","cranial nerve exam",["localization of function","mental status exam","ischemic stroke"],"cranial nerve exam: major section of the neurological exam that assesses sensory and motor functions of the cranial nerves and their associated central and peripheral structures.","The answer begins with C and has 3 words.","fs-id1641247","f-a642ac2cc1b6"],["q16-bff743afaf9b","s16.1-fea06c512aaf","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfluid accumulation in tissue; often associated with circulatory deficits","edema",["hypovolemia","stroke","gait"],"edema: fluid accumulation in tissue; often associated with circulatory deficits.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1989022","f-8aab97939b85"],["q16-a4023391007c","s16.1-fb1a7304cf16","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nobstruction in a blood vessel such as a blood clot, fatty mass, air bubble, or other foreign matter that interrupts the flow of blood to an organ or some part of the body","embolus",["stroke","gait","hypovolemia"],"embolus: obstruction in a blood vessel such as a blood clot, fatty mass, air bubble, or other foreign matter that interrupts the flow of blood to an organ or some part of the body.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1632853","f-1120caadb7aa"],["q16-a337559f53ac","s16.1-bfeeed260fa0","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nrhythmic pattern of alternating movements of the lower limbs during locomotion","gait",["embolus","stroke","hypovolemia"],"gait: rhythmic pattern of alternating movements of the lower limbs during locomotion.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id1641247","f-7c83f4a0e848"],["q16-1be8f575c77a","s16.1-b9828547d9c6","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmajor section of the neurological exam that assesses the cerebellum and descending pathways in the spinal cord through the coordinated motor functions of walking; a portion of the coordination exam","gait exam",["sensory exam","motor exam","hemorrhagic stroke"],"gait exam: major section of the neurological exam that assesses the cerebellum and descending pathways in the spinal cord through the coordinated motor functions of walking; a portion of the coordination exam.","The answer begins with G and has 2 words.","fs-id1641247","f-360dab7757b4"],["q16-438b58473c20","s16.1-56f769351f69","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndisruption of blood flow to the brain caused by bleeding within the cranial vault","hemorrhagic stroke",["motor exam","gait exam","sensory exam"],"hemorrhagic stroke: disruption of blood flow to the brain caused by bleeding within the cranial vault.","The answer begins with H and has 2 words.","fs-id1989022","f-c03f674fe593"],["q16-be4c8345998b","s16.1-14c5081b54f8","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndecrease in blood volume","hypovolemia",["stroke","edema","embolus"],"hypovolemia: decrease in blood volume.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1571384","f-e4e6011e1731"],["q16-7ee97226e853","s16.1-5b797f7ce890","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndisruption of blood flow to the brain because blood cannot flow through blood vessels as a result of a blockage or narrowing of the vessel","ischemic stroke",["coordination exam","hemorrhagic stroke","gait exam"],"ischemic stroke: disruption of blood flow to the brain because blood cannot flow through blood vessels as a result of a blockage or narrowing of the vessel.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id1571384","f-b5b79bfb1be5"],["q16-0a69a0f84173","s16.1-98f437d52f00","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprinciple that circumscribed anatomical locations are responsible for specific functions in an organ system","localization of function",["cranial nerve exam","mental status exam","edema"],"localization of function: principle that circumscribed anatomical locations are responsible for specific functions in an organ system.","The answer begins with L and has 3 words.","fs-id2595084","f-65644e2e36f1"],["q16-db50e2e684d9","s16.1-c0cef9dd1302","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmajor section of the neurological exam that assesses cognitive functions of the cerebrum","mental status exam",["cranial nerve exam","localization of function","edema"],"mental status exam: major section of the neurological exam that assesses cognitive functions of the cerebrum.","The answer begins with M and has 3 words.","fs-id1641247","f-a4a61c88ee76"],["q16-a2c26502d5ae","s16.1-960bebb16499","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmajor section of the neurological exam that assesses motor functions of the spinal cord and spinal nerves","motor exam",["hemorrhagic stroke","ischemic stroke","coordination exam"],"motor exam: major section of the neurological exam that assesses motor functions of the spinal cord and spinal nerves.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id1641247","f-78df9607c99e"],["q16-8d4a3800dc1e","s16.1-d2f8e583a2ca","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmajor section of the neurological exam that assesses sensory functions of the spinal cord and spinal nerves","sensory exam",["coordination exam","gait exam","ischemic stroke"],"sensory exam: major section of the neurological exam that assesses sensory functions of the spinal cord and spinal nerves.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1641247","f-50a841a87299"],["q16-0f10e4126bc0","s16.1-bb5c2a63d939","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntemporary disruption of blood flow to the brain in which symptoms occur rapidly but last only a short time","transient ischemic attack (TIA)",["mental status exam","sensory exam","hemorrhagic stroke"],"transient ischemic attack (TIA): temporary disruption of blood flow to the brain in which symptoms occur rapidly but last only a short time.","The answer begins with T and has 4 words.","fs-id2520527","f-ea41ae41eb04"],["q16-1ea2060e4906","s16.1-404f42c65aaa","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is a clinical assessment tool used to determine what specific parts of the CNS are affected by damage or disease.","neurological exam",["hemorrhagic stroke","motor exam","sensory exam"],"The neurological exam is a clinical assessment tool used to determine what specific parts of the CNS are affected by damage or disease.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2673650","f-2f1eaf53385e"],["q16-9345dc82cb95","s16.1-410666712157","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ tests the sensory and motor functions of each of the nerves, as applicable.","cranial nerve exam",["localization of function","mental status exam","hemorrhagic stroke"],"The cranial nerve exam tests the sensory and motor functions of each of the nerves, as applicable.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1641247","f-6be74cae3b7a"],["q16-1d8e5d62ded0","s16.1-d2f8e583a2ca","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTwo major sections, the ____ and the motor exam, test the sensory and motor functions associated with spinal nerves.","sensory exam",["neurological exam","hemorrhagic stroke","ischemic stroke"],"Two major sections, the sensory exam and the motor exam, test the sensory and motor functions associated with spinal nerves.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1641247","f-2313aaf12c2a"],["q16-62bde5fb444f","s16.1-d807d3ef18b1","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe gait exam, which is often considered a sixth major exam, specifically assesses the motor function of walking and can be considered part of the ____ because walking is a coordinated movement.","coordination exam",["ischemic stroke","sensory exam","neurological exam"],"The gait exam, which is often considered a sixth major exam, specifically assesses the motor function of walking and can be considered part of the coordination exam because walking is a coordinated movement.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1641247","f-b527c75cbcc0"],["q16-c2ec7e00c48a","s16.1-98f437d52f00","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the concept that circumscribed locations are responsible for specific functions.","localization of function",["cranial nerve exam","mental status exam","hemorrhagic stroke"],"Localization of function is the concept that circumscribed locations are responsible for specific functions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2595084","f-e3ceb4900080"],["q16-4c3a42e0c238","s16.1-c0cef9dd1302","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe cognitive functions that are assessed in the ____ are based on functions in the cerebrum, mostly in the cerebral cortex.","mental status exam",["cranial nerve exam","localization of function","transient ischemic attack (TIA)"],"For example, the cognitive functions that are assessed in the mental status exam are based on functions in the cerebrum, mostly in the cerebral cortex.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2595084","f-a4a248833e12"],["q16-6b725b4a0dc3","s16.1-404f42c65aaa","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe five major sections of the ____ are related to the major regions of the CNS.","neurological exam",["ischemic stroke","gait exam","motor exam"],"The five major sections of the neurological exam are related to the major regions of the CNS.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2644060","f-d35ef817c0d9"],["q16-c45247e7175a","s16.1-c0cef9dd1302","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ assesses functions related to the cerebrum.","mental status exam",["localization of function","cranial nerve exam","stroke"],"The mental status exam assesses functions related to the cerebrum.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2644060","f-37728f77c004"],["q16-6248bea0650a","s16.1-410666712157","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is for the nerves that connect to the diencephalon and brain stem (as well as the olfactory connections to the forebrain).","cranial nerve exam",["mental status exam","localization of function","ischemic stroke"],"The cranial nerve exam is for the nerves that connect to the diencephalon and brain stem (as well as the olfactory connections to the forebrain).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2644060","f-10198de9d21f"],["q16-07e3378a74b1","s16.1-d807d3ef18b1","16.1","Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ and the related gait exam primarily assess the functions of the cerebellum.","coordination exam",["sensory exam","ischemic stroke","neurological exam"],"The coordination exam and the related gait exam primarily assess the functions of the cerebellum.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2644060","f-c19520e2e7d8"],["q16-28d3a6f2b414","s16.1-fa2a4fd4647f","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe loss of blood flow to part of the brain is known as a ____, or a cerebrovascular accident (CVA).","stroke",["embolus","hypovolemia","gait"],"The loss of blood flow to part of the brain is known as a stroke, or a cerebrovascular accident (CVA).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1645044","f-84a3adc03999"],["q16-4139a97e82cb","s16.1-fa2a4fd4647f","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThere are two main types of ____, depending on how the blood supply is compromised: ischemic and hemorrhagic.","stroke",["embolus","edema","gait"],"There are two main types of stroke, depending on how the blood supply is compromised: ischemic and hemorrhagic.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1571384","f-8ea774740a74"],["q16-c314dbb3b73d","s16.1-5b797f7ce890","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is the loss of blood flow to an area because vessels are blocked or narrowed.","ischemic stroke",["motor exam","neurological exam","hemorrhagic stroke"],"An ischemic stroke is the loss of blood flow to an area because vessels are blocked or narrowed.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1571384","f-185f39d54d81"],["q16-506a5f1b549e","s16.1-14c5081b54f8","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIschemia may also be the result of thickening of the blood vessel wall, or a drop in blood volume in the brain known as ____.","hypovolemia",["stroke","gait","embolus"],"Ischemia may also be the result of thickening of the blood vessel wall, or a drop in blood volume in the brain known as hypovolemia.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1571384","f-5692aa4674f1"],["q16-9dff7ad79874","s16.1-fa2a4fd4647f","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe diagnostic definition of a ____ includes effects that last at least 24 hours.","stroke",["hypovolemia","embolus","edema"],"The diagnostic definition of a stroke includes effects that last at least 24 hours.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2520527","f-f897b7df0b15"],["q16-f9a7db336046","s16.1-fa2a4fd4647f","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAny ____ symptoms that are resolved within a 24-hour period because of restoration of adequate blood flow are classified as a TIA.","stroke",["edema","embolus","gait"],"Any stroke symptoms that are resolved within a 24-hour period because of restoration of adequate blood flow are classified as a TIA.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2520527","f-ce5323806df6"],["q16-95283a61a823","s16.1-56f769351f69","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is bleeding into the brain because of a damaged blood vessel.","hemorrhagic stroke",["ischemic stroke","neurological exam","gait exam"],"A hemorrhagic stroke is bleeding into the brain because of a damaged blood vessel.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989022","f-1a884034aab7"],["q16-f5b7e9ef9c7c","s16.1-c8c2beeb01a1","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nPhysical pressure on the brain can cause the loss of function, as well as the squeezing of local arteries resulting in compromised ____ beyond the site of the hemorrhage.","blood flow",["gait exam","motor exam","hemorrhagic stroke"],"Physical pressure on the brain can cause the loss of function, as well as the squeezing of local arteries resulting in compromised blood flow beyond the site of the hemorrhage.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989022","f-f1f829bcabc2"],["q16-b9a14ffd7b27","s16.1-fea06c512aaf","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs blood pools in the nervous tissue and the vasculature is damaged, the blood-brain barrier can break down and allow additional fluid to accumulate in the region, which is known as ____.","edema",["hypovolemia","gait","stroke"],"As blood pools in the nervous tissue and the vasculature is damaged, the blood-brain barrier can break down and allow additional fluid to accumulate in the region, which is known as edema.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989022","f-27f625f65fcf"],["q16-d1c4cee40939","s16.1-404f42c65aaa","16.1","Causes of Neurological Deficits","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nInfection, trauma, and congenital disorders can all lead to significant signs, as identified through the ____.","neurological exam",["sensory exam","motor exam","coordination exam"],"Infection, trauma, and congenital disorders can all lead to significant signs, as identified through the neurological exam.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1953003","f-c2a3b68a859d"],["q16-b0c39821464b","s16.1-404f42c65aaa","16.1","Overview of the Neurological Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nStudying the ____ can give insight into how structure and function in the nervous system are interdependent.","neurological exam",["hemorrhagic stroke","gait exam","ischemic stroke"],"Studying the neurological exam can give insight into how structure and function in the nervous system are interdependent.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1414202","f-b7724c67d8a7"],["q16-c07199ca8f89","s16.2-3fe44e80b7a5","16.2","Orientation and Memory","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ninability to form new memories from a particular time forward","anterograde amnesia",["episodic memory","neurological exam","procedural memory"],"anterograde amnesia: inability to form new memories from a particular time forward.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id2464354","f-47b7a5195675"],["q16-aa72e45212ca","s16.2-d784c5c6ec6d","16.2","Language and Speech","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloss of language function","aphasia",["stereognosis","praxis","cytoarchitecture"],"aphasia: loss of language function.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2762781","f-9b9ccb511f53"],["q16-5eb1d321cb51","s16.2-0a0215afd4f4","16.2","Language and Speech","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloss of language function related to connecting the understanding of speech with the production of speech, without either specific function being lost","conduction aphasia",["procedural memory","retrograde amnesia","expressive aphasia"],"conduction aphasia: loss of language function related to connecting the understanding of speech with the production of speech, without either specific function being lost.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1233613","f-7ef1ec5d952e"],["q16-aa910f76d5c2","s16.2-7eb9f64d99d2","16.2","Functions of the Cerebral Cortex","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstudy of a tissue based on the structure and organization of its cellular components; related to the broader term, histology","cytoarchitecture",["gnosis","stereognosis","graphesthesia"],"cytoarchitecture: study of a tissue based on the structure and organization of its cellular components; related to the broader term, histology.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1221013","f-083f2c77ba05"],["q16-3968b3e44dc6","s16.2-67287c6e6e34","16.2","Orientation and Memory","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmemory of specific events in an autobiographical sense","episodic memory",["conduction aphasia","procedural memory","Wernicke’s area"],"episodic memory: memory of specific events in an autobiographical sense.","The answer begins with E and has 2 words.","fs-id2464354","f-50f7d90d4875"],["q16-54f62bfd5858","s16.2-8c74c5fb0cf2","16.2","Language and Speech","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloss of the ability to produce language; usually associated with damage to Broca’s area in the frontal lobe","expressive aphasia",["anterograde amnesia","neurological exam","receptive aphasia"],"expressive aphasia: loss of the ability to produce language; usually associated with damage to Broca’s area in the frontal lobe.","The answer begins with E and has 2 words.","fs-id2762781","f-889d2f4a3d30"],["q16-ad52dd57c608","s16.2-70cdefd9929b","16.2","Sensorium","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nin a neurological exam, intuitive experiential knowledge tested by interacting with common objects or symbols","gnosis",["graphesthesia","praxis","cytoarchitecture"],"gnosis: in a neurological exam, intuitive experiential knowledge tested by interacting with common objects or symbols.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id1266931","f-fece1e85486f"],["q16-09a3ec78cb08","s16.2-e5f91346a7cd","16.2","Sensorium","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nperception of symbols, such as letters or numbers, traced in the palm of the hand","graphesthesia",["stereognosis","gnosis","praxis"],"graphesthesia: perception of symbols, such as letters or numbers, traced in the palm of the hand.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id1266931","f-d1e77542837c"],["q16-b53d1aed5261","s16.2-5ab323a32e0f","16.2","Sensorium","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nin a neurological exam, the act of doing something using ready knowledge or skills in response to verbal instruction","praxis",["aphasia","cytoarchitecture","gnosis"],"praxis: in a neurological exam, the act of doing something using ready knowledge or skills in response to verbal instruction.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id2142396","f-f3ee80fc0a31"],["q16-87c3ae1395aa","s16.2-cafb6f188e7b","16.2","Orientation and Memory","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmemory of how to perform a specific task","procedural memory",["anterograde amnesia","receptive aphasia","neurological exam"],"procedural memory: memory of how to perform a specific task.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id2464354","f-1e890e4abe05"],["q16-52e95494dc3a","s16.2-14f87440b927","16.2","Language and Speech","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloss of the ability to understand received language, such as what is spoken to the subject or given in written form","receptive aphasia",["procedural memory","conduction aphasia","anterograde amnesia"],"receptive aphasia: loss of the ability to understand received language, such as what is spoken to the subject or given in written form.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id2762781","f-640881c35ba4"],["q16-c56829749b5b","s16.2-12d7b0c95076","16.2","Orientation and Memory","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloss of memories before a particular event","retrograde amnesia",["neurological exam","anterograde amnesia","receptive aphasia"],"retrograde amnesia: loss of memories before a particular event.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id2464354","f-0991d2972759"],["q16-1cde7a82826c","s16.2-8d881fd60c43","16.2","Orientation and Memory","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncapacity to retain information actively in the brain for a brief period of time","short-term memory",["anterograde amnesia","expressive aphasia","receptive aphasia"],"short-term memory: capacity to retain information actively in the brain for a brief period of time.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2464354","f-2db1c393a33f"],["q16-dfc4ad0fa694","s16.2-ebb5ea907fb8","16.2","Sensorium","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nperception of common objects placed in the hand solely on the basis of manipulation of that object in the hand","stereognosis",["aphasia","graphesthesia","praxis"],"stereognosis: perception of common objects placed in the hand solely on the basis of manipulation of that object in the hand.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1266931","f-eaff55ca191e"],["q16-e0a7e4a9dce1","s16.2-ac25e64faec0","16.2","Language and Speech","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregion at the posterior end of the lateral sulcus in which speech comprehension is localized","Wernicke’s area",["procedural memory","conduction aphasia","anterograde amnesia"],"Wernicke’s area: region at the posterior end of the lateral sulcus in which speech comprehension is localized.","The answer begins with W and has 2 words.","fs-id2762781","f-d464265c589c"],["q16-004f5c1bfb1e","s16.2-c0cef9dd1302","16.2","The Mental Status Exam","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the clinical setting, the set of subtests known as the ____ helps us understand the relationship of the brain to the body.","mental status exam",["receptive aphasia","aphasia","retrograde amnesia"],"In the clinical setting, the set of subtests known as the mental status exam helps us understand the relationship of the brain to the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2147627","f-0eb78413f934"],["q16-95fe8b20a11d","s16.2-46dff8f1542d","16.2","Functions of the Cerebral Cortex","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe cerebral cortex is the thin layer of gray matter on the outside of the ____.","cerebrum",["graphesthesia","cytoarchitecture","gnosis"],"The cerebral cortex is the thin layer of gray matter on the outside of the cerebrum.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2271786","f-db67103dfddb"],["q16-48a69d547569","s16.2-7eb9f64d99d2","16.2","Functions of the Cerebral Cortex","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe German neurologist and histologist Korbinian Brodmann, who made a careful study of the ____ of the cerebrum around the turn of the nineteenth century, described approximately 50 regions of the cortex that differed enough from each other to be considered separate areas.","cytoarchitecture",["praxis","graphesthesia","gnosis"],"The German neurologist and histologist Korbinian Brodmann, who made a careful study of the cytoarchitecture of the cerebrum around the turn of the nineteenth century, described approximately 50 regions of the cortex that differed enough from each other to be considered separate areas.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1221013","f-41235323c7a0"],["q16-e666e6fcb298","s16.2-e9df3c622253","16.2","Functions of the Cerebral Cortex","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nArea 4 is the primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus, whereas area 6 is the ____.","premotor cortex",["neurological exam","Wernicke’s area","expressive aphasia"],"Area 4 is the primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus, whereas area 6 is the premotor cortex.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1605801","f-470d1bbb0b00"],["q16-ff045ba37e69","s16.2-46b0baa6db41","16.2","Functions of the Cerebral Cortex","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nPrimary areas are where sensory information is initially received from the thalamus for conscious perception, or—in the case of the primary motor cortex—where descending commands are sent down to the ____ or spinal cord to execute movements.","brain stem",["short-term memory","neurological exam","episodic memory"],"Primary areas are where sensory information is initially received from the thalamus for conscious perception, or—in the case of the primary motor cortex—where descending commands are sent down to the brain stem or spinal cord to execute movements.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1236660","f-4ad243f6c5f0"],["q16-6889da650a08","s16.2-c0cef9dd1302","16.2","Cognitive Abilities","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe abilities assessed through the ____ can be separated into four groups: orientation and memory, language and speech, sensorium, and judgment and abstract reasoning.","mental status exam",["neurological exam","graphesthesia","praxis"],"The abilities assessed through the mental status exam can be separated into four groups: orientation and memory, language and speech, sensorium, and judgment and abstract reasoning.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282936","f-e090d7203c57"],["q16-8ecbbabc0272","s16.2-3fe44e80b7a5","16.2","Orientation and Memory","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn 1953, a bilateral lobectomy was performed that alleviated the epilepsy but resulted in the inability for HM to form new memories—a condition called ____.","anterograde amnesia",["neurological exam","conduction aphasia","expressive aphasia"],"In 1953, a bilateral lobectomy was performed that alleviated the epilepsy but resulted in the inability for HM to form new memories—a condition called anterograde amnesia.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2464354","f-2980c6c47c24"],["q16-5852be4e4033","s16.2-c0cef9dd1302","16.2","Orientation and Memory","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn one subtest of the ____ called set generation, the patient is asked to generate a list of words that all start with the same letter, but not to include proper nouns or names.","mental status exam",["cytoarchitecture","Wernicke’s area","retrograde amnesia"],"In one subtest of the mental status exam called set generation, the patient is asked to generate a list of words that all start with the same letter, but not to include proper nouns or names.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1921609","f-3a5e36bda9b4"],["q16-d6f7b4eea9f4","s16.2-c0cef9dd1302","16.2","The Mental Status Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAt the hospital, a neurologist administers the ____, which is mostly normal except for the three-word recall test.","mental status exam",["retrograde amnesia","gnosis","short-term memory"],"At the hospital, a neurologist administers the mental status exam, which is mostly normal except for the three-word recall test.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2320687","f-69408e12b4ce"],["q16-c664f47cd783","s16.2-404f42c65aaa","16.2","Language and Speech","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ has two specific subtests that address language.","neurological exam",["short-term memory","procedural memory","retrograde amnesia"],"The neurological exam has two specific subtests that address language.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2723899","f-e156567312b9"],["q16-9ce0d8b01fda","s16.2-ac25e64faec0","16.2","Language and Speech","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAdjacent to the auditory association cortex, at the end of the lateral sulcus just anterior to the visual cortex, is ____.","Wernicke’s area",["short-term memory","episodic memory","receptive aphasia"],"Adjacent to the auditory association cortex, at the end of the lateral sulcus just anterior to the visual cortex, is Wernicke’s area.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2762781","f-67fb1637c8f2"],["q16-b9415dbf810a","s16.2-d784c5c6ec6d","16.2","Language and Speech","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBoth regions were originally described on the basis of losses of speech and language, which is called ____.","aphasia",["gnosis","stereognosis","cytoarchitecture"],"Both regions were originally described on the basis of losses of speech and language, which is called aphasia.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2762781","f-1c9322039d4a"],["q16-b962a1c13194","s16.2-8c74c5fb0cf2","16.2","Language and Speech","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe aphasia associated with Broca’s area is known as an ____, which means that speech production is compromised.","expressive aphasia",["procedural memory","Wernicke’s area","receptive aphasia"],"The aphasia associated with Broca’s area is known as an expressive aphasia, which means that speech production is compromised.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2762781","f-3ae1c9cf904f"],["q16-0d23a76fc9f6","s16.2-14f87440b927","16.2","Language and Speech","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe aphasia associated with Wernicke’s area is known as a ____, which is not a loss of speech production, but a loss of understanding of content.","receptive aphasia",["retrograde amnesia","conduction aphasia","procedural memory"],"The aphasia associated with Wernicke’s area is known as a receptive aphasia, which is not a loss of speech production, but a loss of understanding of content.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2762781","f-bd89de6293cb"],["q16-54f979515bd9","s16.2-5ab323a32e0f","16.2","Sensorium","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe first is ____, a practical exercise in which the patient performs a task completely on the basis of verbal description without any demonstration from the examiner.","praxis",["stereognosis","gnosis","cytoarchitecture"],"The first is praxis, a practical exercise in which the patient performs a task completely on the basis of verbal description without any demonstration from the examiner.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2142396","f-d31ab946d308"],["q16-87c17388c52f","s16.2-5ab323a32e0f","16.2","Sensorium","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ and gnosis are related to the conscious perception and cortical processing of sensory information.","Praxis",["graphesthesia","aphasia","stereognosis"],"Praxis and gnosis are related to the conscious perception and cortical processing of sensory information.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1632914","f-530b24b5513e"],["q16-0f838f091e64","s16.2-c0cef9dd1302","16.2","Judgment and Abstract Reasoning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the ____, the subtest that assesses judgment and reasoning is directed at three aspects of frontal lobe function.","mental status exam",["Wernicke’s area","stereognosis","expressive aphasia"],"In the mental status exam, the subtest that assesses judgment and reasoning is directed at three aspects of frontal lobe function.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1432760","f-02da4c9f125b"],["q16-dce4ddb99791","s16.2-e9df3c622253","16.2","Judgment and Abstract Reasoning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAnterior to that are the ____, Broca’s area, and the frontal eye fields, which are all related to planning certain types of movements.","premotor cortex",["Wernicke’s area","receptive aphasia","episodic memory"],"Anterior to that are the premotor cortex, Broca’s area, and the frontal eye fields, which are all related to planning certain types of movements.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2338069","f-74712ad9ade0"],["q16-56a8b6e034da","s16.2-f94953a90cbd","16.2","Left Brain, Right Brain","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs an extreme measure to deal with a debilitating condition, the ____ may be sectioned to overcome intractable epilepsy.","corpus callosum",["expressive aphasia","receptive aphasia","retrograde amnesia"],"As an extreme measure to deal with a debilitating condition, the corpus callosum may be sectioned to overcome intractable epilepsy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1289312","f-86da0555506a"],["q16-495ae0e3e62c","s16.2-f94953a90cbd","16.2","Left Brain, Right Brain","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFor a verbal command to initiate movement of the right arm and hand, the left side of the brain needs to be connected by the ____.","corpus callosum",["anterograde amnesia","short-term memory","episodic memory"],"For a verbal command to initiate movement of the right arm and hand, the left side of the brain needs to be connected by the corpus callosum.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1697696","f-6b76e34736d5"],["q16-9cca3c94b388","s16.3-65b0cfa2ea6d","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nin vision, a change in the ability of the eye to focus on objects at different distances","accommodation",["intorsion","fauces","stroke"],"accommodation: in vision, a change in the ability of the eye to focus on objects at different distances.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2070652","f-bd445e8a6e04"],["q16-6e77bedd89a8","s16.3-14a17e147cb9","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncoordination of somatic control of the medial rectus muscles of either eye with the parasympathetic control of the ciliary bodies to maintain focus while the eyes converge on visual stimuli near to the face","accommodation–convergence reflex",["internuclear ophthalmoplegia","jaw-jerk reflex","Snellen chart"],"accommodation–convergence reflex: coordination of somatic control of the medial rectus muscles of either eye with the parasympathetic control of the ciliary bodies to maintain focus while the eyes converge on visual stimuli near to the face.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id2070652","f-2a7410e48bda"],["q16-d8781bdff833","s16.3-d658cedba483","16.3","Sensory Nerves","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nhearing dependent on the conduction of vibrations of the tympanic membrane through the ossicles of the middle ear","conductive hearing",["Rinne test","conjugate gaze","accommodation–convergence reflex"],"conductive hearing: hearing dependent on the conduction of vibrations of the tympanic membrane through the ossicles of the middle ear.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1962641","f-b23b50a222a7"],["q16-848c1aaf14b5","s16.3-a929e85fcb71","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncoordinated movement of the two eyes simultaneously in the same direction","conjugate gaze",["neurological exam","conductive hearing","motor exam"],"conjugate gaze: coordinated movement of the two eyes simultaneously in the same direction.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2364152","f-e020bfae223a"],["q16-f201398b583d","s16.3-70a2ba4762da","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nin vision, the movement of the eyes so that they are both pointed at the same point in space, which increases for stimuli that are closer to the subject","convergence",["stroke","diplopia","intorsion"],"convergence: in vision, the movement of the eyes so that they are both pointed at the same point in space, which increases for stimuli that are closer to the subject.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2070652","f-63841914cc72"],["q16-e431c2be6d05","s16.3-b12728338ca7","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndouble vision resulting from a failure in conjugate gaze","diplopia",["fauces","convergence","gait"],"diplopia: double vision resulting from a failure in conjugate gaze.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1475678","f-985a7a8b2d88"],["q16-75e57daccee8","s16.3-cf486f93ee6e","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscles that are connected to other structures, such as the hyoid bone or the mandible, and control the position of the tongue","extrinsic muscles of the tongue",["paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF)","intrinsic muscles of the tongue","cranial nerve exam"],"extrinsic muscles of the tongue: muscles that are connected to other structures, such as the hyoid bone or the mandible, and control the position of the tongue.","The answer begins with E and has 5 words.","fs-id1865012","f-278c11d9a12d"],["q16-cf915849a159","s16.3-dacd0f9edc35","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nopening from the oral cavity into the pharynx","fauces",["diplopia","gait","saccade"],"fauces: opening from the oral cavity into the pharynx.","The answer begins with F and has 1 word.","fs-id2307861","f-828268ac90e4"],["q16-f5884a4dc736","s16.3-edca23e8bb3a","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndeficit of conjugate lateral gaze because the lateral rectus muscle of one eye does not contract resulting from damage to the abducens nerve or the MLF","internuclear ophthalmoplegia",["sensory exam","jaw-jerk reflex","Rinne test"],"internuclear ophthalmoplegia: deficit of conjugate lateral gaze because the lateral rectus muscle of one eye does not contract resulting from damage to the abducens nerve or the MLF.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id1475678","f-299df8d8ff8d"],["q16-e4f23dfcdfc9","s16.3-2b5fc8a764d8","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmedial rotation of the eye around its axis","intorsion",["stroke","fauces","saccade"],"intorsion: medial rotation of the eye around its axis.","The answer begins with I and has 1 word.","fs-id2337668","f-1cfa36a23714"],["q16-043f8981f741","s16.3-f1ee8cc498a3","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscles that originate out of, and insert into, other tissues within the tongue and control the shape of the tongue","intrinsic muscles of the tongue",["extrinsic muscles of the tongue","paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF)","cranial nerve exam"],"intrinsic muscles of the tongue: muscles that originate out of, and insert into, other tissues within the tongue and control the shape of the tongue.","The answer begins with I and has 5 words.","fs-id1865012","f-cc5090f7ac9e"],["q16-453d3d7fa1a4","s16.3-682ebc0304b8","16.3","Sensory Nerves","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstretch reflex of the masseter muscle","jaw-jerk reflex",["Romberg test","Snellen chart","conductive hearing"],"jaw-jerk reflex: stretch reflex of the masseter muscle.","The answer begins with J and has 2 words.","fs-id2427159","f-430032d0dcd6"],["q16-71305023572a","s16.3-6c07288fb8a4","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfiber pathway that connects structures involved in the control of eye and head position, from the superior colliculus to the vestibular nuclei and cerebellum","medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)",["conductive hearing","intrinsic muscles of the tongue","motor exam"],"medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF): fiber pathway that connects structures involved in the control of eye and head position, from the superior colliculus to the vestibular nuclei and cerebellum.","The answer begins with M and has 4 words.","fs-id2364152","f-29205ac84025"],["q16-5b30e15e3b90","s16.3-9f630f0ba4af","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregion of the brain stem adjacent to the motor nuclei for gaze control that coordinates rapid, conjugate eye movements","paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF)",["extrinsic muscles of the tongue","intrinsic muscles of the tongue","vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR)"],"paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF): region of the brain stem adjacent to the motor nuclei for gaze control that coordinates rapid, conjugate eye movements.","The answer begins with P and has 5 words.","fs-id2364152","f-e065d3585f39"],["q16-1fbea32b03e7","s16.3-d4a0ecf8c20f","16.3","Sensory Nerves","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nuse of a tuning fork to test conductive hearing loss versus sensorineural hearing loss","Rinne test",["Romberg test","jaw-jerk reflex","neurological exam"],"Rinne test: use of a tuning fork to test conductive hearing loss versus sensorineural hearing loss.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id1962641","f-9e99794a3fe5"],["q16-e194308955ee","s16.3-ecf285d740ab","16.3","Sensory Nerves","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntest of equilibrium that requires the patient to maintain a straight, upright posture without visual feedback of position","Romberg test",["sensory exam","Rinne test","jaw-jerk reflex"],"Romberg test: test of equilibrium that requires the patient to maintain a straight, upright posture without visual feedback of position.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id1520520","f-9ffe0833038e"],["q16-365dbf19e2da","s16.3-dc518b0f2dc4","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmall, rapid movement of the eyes used to locate and direct the fovea onto visual stimuli","saccade",["stroke","diplopia","fauces"],"saccade: small, rapid movement of the eyes used to locate and direct the fovea onto visual stimuli.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2364152","f-17406b6be882"],["q16-100b94e14b48","s16.3-36e9eb6a2866","16.3","Sensory Nerves","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nhearing dependent on the transduction and propagation of auditory information through the neural components of the peripheral auditory structures","sensorineural hearing",["neurological exam","jaw-jerk reflex","Weber test"],"sensorineural hearing: hearing dependent on the transduction and propagation of auditory information through the neural components of the peripheral auditory structures.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1962641","f-77d8aee42758"],["q16-f876268889c6","s16.3-4c5bb5f8e2d3","16.3","Sensory Nerves","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstandardized arrangement of letters in decreasing size presented to a subject at a distance of 20 feet to test visual acuity","Snellen chart",["accommodation–convergence reflex","conductive hearing","sensorineural hearing"],"Snellen chart: standardized arrangement of letters in decreasing size presented to a subject at a distance of 20 feet to test visual acuity.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1961926","f-3cc69e115473"],["q16-14249e000a40","s16.3-6241e923bdbf","16.3","Gaze Control","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nreflex based on connections between the vestibular system and the cranial nerves of eye movements that ensures that images are stabilized on the retina as the head and body move","vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR)",["cranial nerve exam","accommodation","convergence"],"vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR): reflex based on connections between the vestibular system and the cranial nerves of eye movements that ensures that images are stabilized on the retina as the head and body move.","The answer begins with V and has 3 words.","fs-id1984224","f-9053c702a5c5"],["q16-5cb6f676a3f8","s16.3-e643f07afada","16.3","Sensory Nerves","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nuse of a tuning fork to test the laterality of hearing loss by placing it at several locations on the midline of the skull","Weber test",["conductive hearing","motor exam","accommodation–convergence reflex"],"Weber test: use of a tuning fork to test the laterality of hearing loss by placing it at several locations on the midline of the skull.","The answer begins with W and has 2 words.","fs-id1962641","f-9e59948e7704"],["q16-b92409af8e4d","s16.3-410666712157","16.3","The Cranial Nerve Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ allows directed tests of forebrain and brain stem structures.","cranial nerve exam",["vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR)","gait","extrinsic muscles of the tongue"],"The cranial nerve exam allows directed tests of forebrain and brain stem structures.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1882311","f-e6f8bc5823f0"],["q16-a14f030d4d47","s16.3-ea98e70a0b4f","16.3","The Cranial Nerve Exam","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ (cranial nerve X) has autonomic functions in the thoracic and superior abdominal cavities.","vagus nerve",["sensorineural hearing","Rinne test","jaw-jerk reflex"],"The vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) has autonomic functions in the thoracic and superior abdominal cavities.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1882311","f-f0b8e9c1b527"],["q16-eda2f0f10d4b","s16.3-255790fdd5bd","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe olfactory, optic, and vestibulocochlear nerves (cranial nerves I, II, and VIII) are dedicated to four of the special senses: smell, vision, ____, and hearing, respectively.","equilibrium",["gait","fauces","stroke"],"The olfactory, optic, and vestibulocochlear nerves (cranial nerves I, II, and VIII) are dedicated to four of the special senses: smell, vision, equilibrium, and hearing, respectively.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1540714","f-8318392a728b"],["q16-9798c18cfd8f","s16.3-3c29c1e7ee89","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe neurons in the ____ have a limited life span, and new cells grow to replace the ones that die off.","olfactory epithelium",["conjugate gaze","motor exam","intrinsic muscles of the tongue"],"The neurons in the olfactory epithelium have a limited life span, and new cells grow to replace the ones that die off.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1978067","f-633b65867cde"],["q16-e06dc44d6890","s16.3-4c5bb5f8e2d3","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ demonstrates visual acuity by presenting standard Roman letters in a variety of sizes.","Snellen chart",["sensorineural hearing","conjugate gaze","Weber test"],"The Snellen chart demonstrates visual acuity by presenting standard Roman letters in a variety of sizes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1961926","f-6ae2fe81ba14"],["q16-feade271a9ac","s16.3-625f139d6ccd","16.3","Sensory Nerves","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLoss of peripheral ____ may be the result of a pituitary tumor pressing on the optic chiasm.","vision",["intorsion","convergence","fauces"],"For example, loss of peripheral vision may be the result of a pituitary tumor pressing on the optic chiasm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2072040","f-cd625bdaea50"],["q16-d6d3dadae2c6","s16.3-4a6e9e5e6f81","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe pituitary, seated in the ____ of the sphenoid bone, is directly inferior to the optic chiasm.","sella turcica",["Snellen chart","conductive hearing","sensorineural hearing"],"The pituitary, seated in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, is directly inferior to the optic chiasm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2072040","f-a1204f041925"],["q16-69cb78155a7c","s16.3-a6a5b795304c","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThough the two senses are not directly related, ____ is mirrored in the two systems.","anatomy",["accommodation","saccade","convergence"],"Though the two senses are not directly related, anatomy is mirrored in the two systems.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1226920","f-82d8a52ebb79"],["q16-80ee72ad623a","s16.3-255790fdd5bd","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe vestibule is the portion for ____, composed of the utricle, saccule, and the three semicircular canals.","equilibrium",["gait","stroke","saccade"],"The vestibule is the portion for equilibrium, composed of the utricle, saccule, and the three semicircular canals.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1226920","f-30162c5b4476"],["q16-07f311b51159","s16.3-63bc19d5329d","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is responsible for transducing sound waves into a neural signal.","cochlea",["intorsion","accommodation","saccade"],"The cochlea is responsible for transducing sound waves into a neural signal.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1226920","f-9fa68993e57c"],["q16-9737d84a6d95","s16.3-255790fdd5bd","16.3","Sensory Nerves","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMénière's disease is a disorder that can affect both ____ and audition in a variety of ways.","equilibrium",["saccade","stroke","fauces"],"Ménière's disease is a disorder that can affect both equilibrium and audition in a variety of ways.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2157619","f-8f988ce564cc"],["q16-1989207185f8","s16.3-404f42c65aaa","16.3","Sensory Nerves","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nUse of the ____ subtests for the vestibulocochlear nerve illuminates the changes a patient may go through.","neurological exam",["Romberg test","internuclear ophthalmoplegia","motor exam"],"Use of the neurological exam subtests for the vestibulocochlear nerve illuminates the changes a patient may go through.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2157619","f-e9403a9c43ca"],["q16-8d735bf0b3d8","s16.3-404f42c65aaa","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTests of equilibrium are important for coordination and gait and are related to other aspects of the ____.","neurological exam",["Snellen chart","Rinne test","spasticity"],"Tests of equilibrium are important for coordination and gait and are related to other aspects of the neurological exam.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1520520","f-25cf1c0c007e"],["q16-dd10b481bf5b","s16.3-36e9eb6a2866","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe Rinne test involves using a tuning fork to distinguish between conductive hearing and ____.","sensorineural hearing",["conjugate gaze","Snellen chart","Weber test"],"The Rinne test involves using a tuning fork to distinguish between conductive hearing and sensorineural hearing.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962641","f-1e2427b58c8b"],["q16-b0496be828f2","s16.3-d658cedba483","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ relies on vibrations being conducted through the ossicles of the middle ear.","conductive hearing",["internuclear ophthalmoplegia","sensory exam","Rinne test"],"Conductive hearing relies on vibrations being conducted through the ossicles of the middle ear.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962641","f-cd1756b99fed"],["q16-975b8ca31334","s16.3-36e9eb6a2866","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the transmission of sound stimuli through the neural components of the inner ear and cranial nerve.","sensorineural hearing",["jaw-jerk reflex","motor exam","sensory exam"],"Sensorineural hearing is the transmission of sound stimuli through the neural components of the inner ear and cranial nerve.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962641","f-2c14574d9375"],["q16-1d6b61fa82e4","s16.3-d658cedba483","16.3","Sensory Nerves","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf the sound is not heard through the ear, meaning the sound is conducted better through the temporal bone than through the ossicles, a ____ deficit is present.","conductive hearing",["Rinne test","sensorineural hearing","jaw-jerk reflex"],"If the sound is not heard through the ear, meaning the sound is conducted better through the temporal bone than through the ossicles, a conductive hearing deficit is present.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962641","f-e318af935e50"],["q16-b882cf909346","s16.3-0f0f752070f2","16.3","Sensory Nerves","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe chief nucleus, located in the pons, receives information about light touch as well as proprioceptive information about the ____, which are both relayed to the thalamus and, ultimately, to the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe.","mandible",["fauces","diplopia","accommodation"],"The chief nucleus, located in the pons, receives information about light touch as well as proprioceptive information about the mandible, which are both relayed to the thalamus and, ultimately, to the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2427159","f-55f7292529f7"],["q16-7a2472c1c381","s16.3-4a7986abc4e0","16.3","Sensory Nerves","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe patient must identify whether the ____ is sharp or dull.","stimulus",["saccade","accommodation","gait"],"The patient must identify whether the stimulus is sharp or dull.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2640277","f-80b01af7074f"],["q16-fdfc40be27a8","s16.3-4a7986abc4e0","16.3","Sensory Nerves","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf a patient cannot recognize a painful ____, that might indicate damage to the spinal trigeminal nucleus in the medulla.","stimulus",["gait","fauces","diplopia"],"If a patient cannot recognize a painful stimulus, that might indicate damage to the spinal trigeminal nucleus in the medulla.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2640277","f-b51a1e098ae6"],["q16-7534231c94a6","s16.3-46b0baa6db41","16.3","Gaze Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCoordinated movement of both eyes through different nuclei requires integrated processing through the ____.","brain stem",["motor exam","Rinne test","conductive hearing"],"Coordinated movement of both eyes through different nuclei requires integrated processing through the brain stem.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2364152","f-12c45ba726dd"],["q16-f9c6eeb0eb4a","s16.3-9f630f0ba4af","16.3","Gaze Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ will initiate a rapid eye movement, or saccade, to bring the eyes to bear on a visual stimulus quickly.","paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF)",["extrinsic muscles of the tongue","intrinsic muscles of the tongue","cranial nerve exam"],"The paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF) will initiate a rapid eye movement, or saccade, to bring the eyes to bear on a visual stimulus quickly.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2364152","f-cbfa1c33a974"],["q16-781d1bbf0d51","s16.3-a929e85fcb71","16.3","Gaze Control","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe MLF allows for ____, or the movement of the eyes in the same direction, during horizontal movements that require the lateral and medial rectus muscles.","conjugate gaze",["jaw-jerk reflex","conductive hearing","sensory exam"],"The MLF allows for conjugate gaze, or the movement of the eyes in the same direction, during horizontal movements that require the lateral and medial rectus muscles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2364152","f-133295a417b2"],["q16-be5146db0ef1","s16.3-a929e85fcb71","16.3","Gaze Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nControl of ____ strictly in the vertical direction is contained within the oculomotor complex.","conjugate gaze",["internuclear ophthalmoplegia","Romberg test","conductive hearing"],"Control of conjugate gaze strictly in the vertical direction is contained within the oculomotor complex.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2364152","f-88b4dd300fca"],["q16-c260eb0454f7","s16.3-dc518b0f2dc4","16.3","Gaze Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe rapid movement of the eyes used to locate and direct the fovea onto visual stimuli is called a ____.","saccade",["stroke","gait","accommodation"],"The rapid movement of the eyes used to locate and direct the fovea onto visual stimuli is called a saccade.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2328919","f-0f9148367598"],["q16-35e2c4c222ab","s16.3-b7e414b0b973","16.3","Gaze Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTo keep the stimulus in focus, the eye also needs to change the shape of the lens, which is controlled through the parasympathetic fibers of the ____.","oculomotor nerve",["Romberg test","Rinne test","Weber test"],"To keep the stimulus in focus, the eye also needs to change the shape of the lens, which is controlled through the parasympathetic fibers of the oculomotor nerve.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2070652","f-fb7dd6de29fc"],["q16-0c756726642c","s16.3-65b0cfa2ea6d","16.3","Gaze Control","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe change in focal power of the eye is referred to as ____.","accommodation",["fauces","saccade","stroke"],"The change in focal power of the eye is referred to as accommodation.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2070652","f-3c81b81bc26f"],["q16-287939f72e5d","s16.3-ab47054b5da4","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAlong with taste, the ____ relays general sensations from the pharyngeal walls.","glossopharyngeal nerve",["conductive hearing","Romberg test","conjugate gaze"],"Along with taste, the glossopharyngeal nerve relays general sensations from the pharyngeal walls.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2302120","f-ce5e8bf98373"],["q16-01badf609aa1","s16.3-dacd0f9edc35","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nStimulation of either side of the ____ should elicit an equivalent response.","fauces",["saccade","gait","accommodation"],"Stimulation of either side of the fauces should elicit an equivalent response.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2302120","f-fbd79cf91f78"],["q16-a3062077a0c1","s16.3-ea98e70a0b4f","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNormally, the ____ is considered autonomic in nature.","vagus nerve",["jaw-jerk reflex","conjugate gaze","Rinne test"],"Normally, the vagus nerve is considered autonomic in nature.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2302120","f-5e5ad586a22e"],["q16-78ee8d6e0bf5","s16.3-dacd0f9edc35","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFurther testing of vagus motor function has the patient repeating consonant sounds that require movement of the muscles around the ____.","fauces",["intorsion","saccade","diplopia"],"Further testing of vagus motor function has the patient repeating consonant sounds that require movement of the muscles around the fauces.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2302120","f-025ae33f702a"],["q16-00b755680d3c","s16.3-cf486f93ee6e","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ are connected to other structures, whereas the intrinsic muscles of the tongue are completely contained within the lingual tissues.","extrinsic muscles of the tongue",["paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF)","medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)","cranial nerve exam"],"The extrinsic muscles of the tongue are connected to other structures, whereas the intrinsic muscles of the tongue are completely contained within the lingual tissues.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1865012","f-bf7f1417b957"],["q16-94a45940adf9","s16.3-1c41cb7cffb7","16.3","Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAdditionally, because the location of the hypoglossal nerve and nucleus is near the ____, inspiratory and expiratory areas for respiration, and the vagus nuclei that regulate digestive functions, a tongue that protrudes incorrectly can suggest damage in adjacent structures that have nothing to do with controlling the tongue.","cardiovascular center",["sensorineural hearing","internuclear ophthalmoplegia","Weber test"],"Additionally, because the location of the hypoglossal nerve and nucleus is near the cardiovascular center, inspiratory and expiratory areas for respiration, and the vagus nuclei that regulate digestive functions, a tongue that protrudes incorrectly can suggest damage in adjacent structures that have nothing to do with controlling the tongue.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1865012","f-bc5ddf99bdc4"],["q16-0a5e66cd76bb","s16.3-2e89b56f2a87","16.3","Motor Nerves of the Neck","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe trapezius can act as an antagonist, causing extension and ____ of the neck.","hyperextension",["saccade","intorsion","fauces"],"The trapezius can act as an antagonist, causing extension and hyperextension of the neck.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1253895","f-f4dcef8f14d6"],["q16-26ea84c3f24e","s16.4-e4d753b53aa6","16.4","Reflexes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndorsiflexion of the foot with extension and splaying of the toes in response to the plantar reflex, normally suppressed by corticospinal input","Babinski sign",["motor exam","Romberg test","superficial reflex"],"Babinski sign: dorsiflexion of the foot with extension and splaying of the toes in response to the plantar reflex, normally suppressed by corticospinal input.","The answer begins with B and has 2 words.","fs-id1907883","f-f29805e1c923"],["q16-6bcc0fa7f03c","s16.4-266d52baebf7","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsign of UMN disease when a patient initially resists passive movement of a muscle but will quickly release to a lower state of resistance","clasp-knife response",["superficial reflex","spinocerebellar tract","flaccid paralysis"],"clasp-knife response: sign of UMN disease when a patient initially resists passive movement of a muscle but will quickly release to a lower state of resistance.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1376888","f-256c0d8f157d"],["q16-8e40425ec336","s16.4-a5f0ec68dec9","16.4","Reflexes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nanother term for stretch reflex, based on the elicitation through deep stimulation of the tendon at the insertion","deep tendon reflex",["mental status exam","motor exam","sensory exam"],"deep tendon reflex: another term for stretch reflex, based on the elicitation through deep stimulation of the tendon at the insertion.","The answer begins with D and has 3 words.","fs-id1640836","f-d686fff62916"],["q16-056c8c2b5120","s16.4-16a5a170140b","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmall muscle twitch as a result of spontaneous activity from an LMN","fasciculation",["stroke","hypotonicity","paresis"],"fasciculation: small muscle twitch as a result of spontaneous activity from an LMN.","The answer begins with F and has 1 word.","fs-id2623052","f-cd914a3dd525"],["q16-a08acb9e6d73","s16.4-67d2b3fcf1ef","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nin motor responses, a spontaneous muscle action potential that occurs in the absence of neuromuscular input, resulting from LMN lesions","fibrillation",["spasticity","stroke","graphesthesia"],"fibrillation: in motor responses, a spontaneous muscle action potential that occurs in the absence of neuromuscular input, resulting from LMN lesions.","The answer begins with F and has 1 word.","fs-id2623052","f-5fef44b41f00"],["q16-b3f3dba0d4e1","s16.4-07bf5bd74167","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloss of voluntary muscle control and muscle tone, as the result of LMN disease","flaccid paralysis",["clasp-knife response","pronator drift","motor exam"],"flaccid paralysis: loss of voluntary muscle control and muscle tone, as the result of LMN disease.","The answer begins with F and has 2 words.","fs-id2623052","f-e132b4cb7ffc"],["q16-d7e4b75678dc","s16.4-e95986ca42d2","16.4","Muscle Strength and Voluntary Movement","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\npresentation of a loss of muscle tone, observed as floppy limbs or a lack of resistance to passive movement","flaccidity",["fibrillation","spasticity","hemisection"],"flaccidity: presentation of a loss of muscle tone, observed as floppy limbs or a lack of resistance to passive movement.","The answer begins with F and has 1 word.","fs-id1967110","f-55abb21dcdd5"],["q16-c2be48c0b2ac","s16.4-74a63c87cf5c","16.4","Sensory Modalities and Location","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncut through half of a structure, such as the spinal cord","hemisection",["hypotonicity","fibrillation","spasticity"],"hemisection: cut through half of a structure, such as the spinal cord.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1339554","f-2720e33131ae"],["q16-27fa1254593e","s16.4-c88c3b704157","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\noverly flexed joints","hyperflexia",["fasciculation","graphesthesia","paresis"],"hyperflexia: overly flexed joints.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1376888","f-6e1309ab461f"],["q16-af79a3fb5cd1","s16.4-189203383466","16.4","Muscle Strength and Voluntary Movement","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlow muscle tone, a sign of LMN disease","hypotonicity",["graphesthesia","fibrillation","hemisection"],"hypotonicity: low muscle tone, a sign of LMN disease.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1967110","f-2f73f76609bd"],["q16-9875c61ebd5f","s16.4-2f18de465514","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\npartial loss of, or impaired, voluntary muscle control","paresis",["fibrillation","hemisection","hyperflexia"],"paresis: partial loss of, or impaired, voluntary muscle control.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id2623052","f-351b4fc21742"],["q16-51deeabe1bf0","s16.4-19da0a785efd","16.4","Reflexes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsuperficial reflex initiated by gentle stimulation of the sole of the foot","plantar reflex",["motor exam","pronator drift","Romberg test"],"plantar reflex: superficial reflex initiated by gentle stimulation of the sole of the foot.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id1907883","f-0c823361dd8e"],["q16-243f9463b73e","s16.4-88710823921d","16.4","Muscle Strength and Voluntary Movement","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsign of contralateral corticospinal lesion when the one arm will drift into a pronated position when held straight out with the palms facing upward","pronator drift",["Babinski sign","flaccid paralysis","plantar reflex"],"pronator drift: sign of contralateral corticospinal lesion when the one arm will drift into a pronated position when held straight out with the palms facing upward.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id2292530","f-b30e72135c51"],["q16-a2bab2c8e1f5","s16.4-f7813d48ce38","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nincreased contraction of a muscle in response to resistance, often resulting in hyperflexia","spasticity",["fibrillation","hemisection","stereognosis"],"spasticity: increased contraction of a muscle in response to resistance, often resulting in hyperflexia.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1376888","f-cb334903bba2"],["q16-666baff85aa1","s16.4-828c047f14f5","16.4","Sensory Modalities and Location","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nascending fibers that carry proprioceptive input to the cerebellum used in maintaining balance and coordinated movement","spinocerebellar tract",["motor exam","superficial reflex","sensory exam"],"spinocerebellar tract: ascending fibers that carry proprioceptive input to the cerebellum used in maintaining balance and coordinated movement.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2242057","f-12617abae418"],["q16-dd587a4f66ac","s16.4-49194ca8bafc","16.4","Reflexes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nreflexive contraction initiated by gentle stimulation of the skin","superficial reflex",["sensory exam","Romberg test","motor exam"],"superficial reflex: reflexive contraction initiated by gentle stimulation of the skin.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1640836","f-521e4705c648"],["q16-50f6d3939b0c","s16.4-a1c05744814f","16.4","Sensory Modalities and Location","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTesting of the senses begins with examining the regions known as dermatomes that connect to the cortical region where ____ is perceived in the postcentral gyrus.","somatosensation",["paresis","hemisection","hyperflexia"],"Testing of the senses begins with examining the regions known as dermatomes that connect to the cortical region where somatosensation is perceived in the postcentral gyrus.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1592604","f-6e30231543e5"],["q16-9dfd0e64f891","s16.4-50e8b9d079bd","16.4","Sensory Modalities and Location","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe fibers of eighth cervical nerve innervate the medial surface of the ____ and extend out to the fingers.","forearm",["stereognosis","hyperflexia","hemisection"],"For example, the fibers of eighth cervical nerve innervate the medial surface of the forearm and extend out to the fingers.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1592604","f-28f34c8c52dc"],["q16-2e70a2b97ad1","s16.4-ecf285d740ab","16.4","Sensory Modalities and Location","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA final subtest of sensory perception that concentrates on the sense of proprioception is known as the ____.","Romberg test",["Babinski sign","pronator drift","spinocerebellar tract"],"A final subtest of sensory perception that concentrates on the sense of proprioception is known as the Romberg test.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2242057","f-e98c4d366fed"],["q16-f7431543dbb1","s16.4-189203383466","16.4","Muscle Strength and Voluntary Movement","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe lack of muscle tone, known as ____ or flaccidity, may indicate that the LMN is not conducting action potentials that will keep a basal level of acetylcholine in the neuromuscular junction.","hypotonicity",["fasciculation","hyperflexia","paresis"],"The lack of muscle tone, known as hypotonicity or flaccidity, may indicate that the LMN is not conducting action potentials that will keep a basal level of acetylcholine in the neuromuscular junction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1967110","f-e0f6782f189a"],["q16-0021b827b5d2","s16.4-88710823921d","16.4","Muscle Strength and Voluntary Movement","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA sign of UMN lesion is a negative result in the subtest for ____.","pronator drift",["superficial reflex","Babinski sign","neurological exam"],"A sign of UMN lesion is a negative result in the subtest for pronator drift.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2292530","f-615f86dbc80a"],["q16-601b9bf4ca58","s16.4-404f42c65aaa","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe reflexes that are tested in the ____ are classified into two groups.","neurological exam",["superficial reflex","Romberg test","sensory exam"],"The reflexes that are tested in the neurological exam are classified into two groups.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1640836","f-ed3ebe7d406e"],["q16-554f61e129ec","s16.4-49194ca8bafc","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is elicited through gentle stimulation of the skin and causes contraction of the associated muscles.","superficial reflex",["plantar reflex","clasp-knife response","pronator drift"],"A superficial reflex is elicited through gentle stimulation of the skin and causes contraction of the associated muscles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1640836","f-602c623dc0cc"],["q16-271933c5d8b7","s16.4-adddf21c862f","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFor the arm, the common reflexes to test are of the biceps, ____, triceps, and flexors for the digits.","brachioradialis",["spasticity","stroke","paresis"],"For the arm, the common reflexes to test are of the biceps, brachioradialis, triceps, and flexors for the digits.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1956663","f-0a71f58619c3"],["q16-85bfba5cb04b","s16.4-404f42c65aaa","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the context of the ____, reflexes indicate that the LMN is functioning properly.","neurological exam",["Babinski sign","pronator drift","clasp-knife response"],"In the context of the neurological exam, reflexes indicate that the LMN is functioning properly.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1956663","f-3e493cd113cb"],["q16-ad5d067319f2","s16.4-19da0a785efd","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is commonly tested in newborn infants to establish the presence of neuromuscular function.","plantar reflex",["superficial reflex","Babinski sign","spinocerebellar tract"],"The plantar reflex is commonly tested in newborn infants to establish the presence of neuromuscular function.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1907883","f-b758c6b00cc4"],["q16-8e4966551590","s16.4-e4d753b53aa6","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn infant would present a positive ____, meaning the foot dorsiflexes and the toes extend and splay out.","Babinski sign",["plantar reflex","spinocerebellar tract","motor exam"],"An infant would present a positive Babinski sign, meaning the foot dorsiflexes and the toes extend and splay out.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1907883","f-ea63eb2aa6fb"],["q16-bff6040b7d02","s16.4-19da0a785efd","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs a person learns to walk, the ____ changes to cause curling of the toes and a moderate plantar flexion.","plantar reflex",["pronator drift","Babinski sign","superficial reflex"],"As a person learns to walk, the plantar reflex changes to cause curling of the toes and a moderate plantar flexion.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1907883","f-00cd907a6f98"],["q16-eea62b692fab","s16.4-19da0a785efd","16.4","Reflexes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe descending input of the corticospinal tract modifies the response of the ____, meaning that a negative Babinski sign is the expected response in testing the reflex.","plantar reflex",["sensory exam","Romberg test","motor exam"],"The descending input of the corticospinal tract modifies the response of the plantar reflex, meaning that a negative Babinski sign is the expected response in testing the reflex.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1907883","f-6b7d9a640db0"],["q16-3bfc14757879","s16.4-266d52baebf7","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSigns that suggest a UMN lesion include muscle weakness, strong deep tendon reflexes, decreased control of movement or slowness, pronator drift, a positive Babinski sign, spasticity, and the ____.","clasp-knife response",["flaccid paralysis","motor exam","plantar reflex"],"Signs that suggest a UMN lesion include muscle weakness, strong deep tendon reflexes, decreased control of movement or slowness, pronator drift, a positive Babinski sign, spasticity, and the clasp-knife response.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1376888","f-53164842a924"],["q16-1f94d4e93914","s16.4-f7813d48ce38","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is an excess contraction in resistance to stretch.","Spasticity",["hyperflexia","graphesthesia","flaccidity"],"Spasticity is an excess contraction in resistance to stretch.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1376888","f-5ce2c8585e4e"],["q16-14dafbe8af76","s16.4-266d52baebf7","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ occurs when the patient initially resists movement, but then releases, and the joint will quickly flex like a pocket knife closing.","clasp-knife response",["pronator drift","plantar reflex","spinocerebellar tract"],"The clasp-knife response occurs when the patient initially resists movement, but then releases, and the joint will quickly flex like a pocket knife closing.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1376888","f-89c35207f82a"],["q16-b8561a178f24","s16.4-2f18de465514","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA lesion on the LMN would result in paralysis, or at least partial loss of voluntary muscle control, which is known as ____.","paresis",["spasticity","fibrillation","stereognosis"],"A lesion on the LMN would result in paralysis, or at least partial loss of voluntary muscle control, which is known as paresis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2623052","f-d690d4a6b5d1"],["q16-ec450f78025f","s16.4-16a5a170140b","16.4","Comparison of Upper and Lower Motor Neuron Damage","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOther signs of an LMN lesion are fibrillation, ____, and compromised or lost reflexes resulting from the denervation of the muscle fibers.","fasciculation",["paresis","spasticity","flaccidity"],"Other signs of an LMN lesion are fibrillation, fasciculation, and compromised or lost reflexes resulting from the denervation of the muscle fibers.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2623052","f-3d4e19d25fba"],["q16-be9f008abaa8","s16.4-321d6cc5177a","16.4","Spinal Cord","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOn the functional ____, the opposite is true.","leg",["paresis","hyperflexia","flaccidity"],"On the functional leg, the opposite is true.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1902874","f-fe8ebd88c334"],["q16-dd32e2204cf5","s16.4-76518e42b606","16.4","Spinal Cord","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe motor system can indicate the loss of input to the ventral horn in the ____ where motor neurons to the leg are found, but motor function in the trunk is less clear.","lumbar enlargement",["Babinski sign","neurological exam","Romberg test"],"The motor system can indicate the loss of input to the ventral horn in the lumbar enlargement where motor neurons to the leg are found, but motor function in the trunk is less clear.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1289253","f-cedd02d9d0cc"],["q16-af84aa925566","s16.4-74a63c87cf5c","16.4","Spinal Cord","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe pain fibers on the side with motor function cross the midline in the spinal cord and ascend in the contralateral lateral column as far as the ____.","hemisection",["spasticity","fibrillation","flaccidity"],"The pain fibers on the side with motor function cross the midline in the spinal cord and ascend in the contralateral lateral column as far as the hemisection.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1953629","f-50f2b84dbb8e"],["q16-802ddcbbb9c8","s16.5-245ac772a2ca","16.5","Ataxia","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmovement disorder related to damage of the cerebellum characterized by loss of coordination in voluntary movements","ataxia",["gait","vermis","vestibulocerebellum"],"ataxia: movement disorder related to damage of the cerebellum characterized by loss of coordination in voluntary movements.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id1339156","f-9c8d4111fa93"],["q16-88cf75c38c51","s16.5-13d6e89b23a2","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlateral regions of the cerebellum; named for the significant input from the cerebral cortex","cerebrocerebellum",["vestibulocerebellum","spinocerebellum","ataxia"],"cerebrocerebellum: lateral regions of the cerebellum; named for the significant input from the cerebral cortex.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2152546","f-7bc84b4af72c"],["q16-8f7fa71ce69b","s16.5-fa8aec744675","16.5","Coordination and Alternating Movement","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nresponse to a release in resistance so that the contractions stop, or check, movement","check reflex",["flocculonodular lobe","rubrospinal tract","spinocerebellar tract"],"check reflex: response to a release in resistance so that the contractions stop, or check, movement.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2306534","f-05a8debc163e"],["q16-1bc944c3101b","s16.5-99df7bbfcf11","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprojection from the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum by way of the gray matter of the pons","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway",["flocculonodular lobe","check reflex","Romberg test"],"cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway: projection from the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum by way of the gray matter of the pons.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2307969","f-32f62437c973"],["q16-b14a50344418","s16.5-921cad7aff32","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlobe of the cerebellum that receives input from the vestibular system to help with balance and posture","flocculonodular lobe",["spinocerebellar tract","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway","red nucleus"],"flocculonodular lobe: lobe of the cerebellum that receives input from the vestibular system to help with balance and posture.","The answer begins with F and has 2 words.","fs-id2152546","f-6ff468bbe77f"],["q16-e3d4821dc91f","s16.5-d9f14fc88fa0","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ninput to the cerebellum, largely from the inferior olive, that represents sensory feedback from the periphery","inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP)",["middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP)","superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP)","rubrospinal tract"],"inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP): input to the cerebellum, largely from the inferior olive, that represents sensory feedback from the periphery.","The answer begins with I and has 4 words.","fs-id2252793","f-07d90f9098e6"],["q16-3f7a4400e958","s16.5-9db525475810","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlarge nucleus in the medulla that receives input from sensory systems and projects into the cerebellar cortex","inferior olive",["spinocerebellar tract","procedural memory","coordination exam"],"inferior olive: large nucleus in the medulla that receives input from sensory systems and projects into the cerebellar cortex.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fig-ch16_05_01","f-53df4e121b07"],["q16-409e7048cc1d","s16.5-ab58ac90afd7","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlarge, white-matter bridge from the pons that constitutes the major input to the cerebellar cortex","middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP)",["inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP)","superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP)","flocculonodular lobe"],"middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP): large, white-matter bridge from the pons that constitutes the major input to the cerebellar cortex.","The answer begins with M and has 4 words.","fs-id2252793","f-c70b9d9ed69c"],["q16-c0cfb0a30ea1","s16.5-7a388262398f","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnucleus in the midbrain that receives output from the cerebellum and projects onto the spinal cord in the rubrospinal tract","red nucleus",["procedural memory","neurological exam","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway"],"red nucleus: nucleus in the midbrain that receives output from the cerebellum and projects onto the spinal cord in the rubrospinal tract.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id2307969","f-2e579ff15099"],["q16-b1067d2dd88f","s16.5-497979e5c82a","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescending tract from the red nucleus of the midbrain that results in modification of ongoing motor programs","rubrospinal tract",["check reflex","flocculonodular lobe","spinocerebellar tract"],"rubrospinal tract: descending tract from the red nucleus of the midbrain that results in modification of ongoing motor programs.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id2307969","f-b1a0d1367edb"],["q16-99b4f3fcd43d","s16.5-bd19bc75b02b","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmidline region of the cerebellum known as the vermis that receives proprioceptive input from the spinal cord","spinocerebellum",["vestibulocerebellum","cerebrocerebellum","gait"],"spinocerebellum: midline region of the cerebellum known as the vermis that receives proprioceptive input from the spinal cord.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2152546","f-73ffa1b0e78d"],["q16-e0183ac6fa57","s16.5-7df96ac8bc32","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nwhite-matter tract representing output of the cerebellum to the red nucleus of the midbrain","superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP)",["middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP)","inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP)","flocculonodular lobe"],"superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP): white-matter tract representing output of the cerebellum to the red nucleus of the midbrain.","The answer begins with S and has 4 words.","fs-id2252793","f-bd609cb1e283"],["q16-04f5cd7b9177","s16.5-b77f5007c565","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprominent ridge along the midline of the cerebellum that is referred to as the spinocerebellum","vermis",["cerebrocerebellum","gait","vestibulocerebellum"],"vermis: prominent ridge along the midline of the cerebellum that is referred to as the spinocerebellum.","The answer begins with V and has 1 word.","fs-id2152546","f-caf6c6d7f7b7"],["q16-77084150c283","s16.5-48a88d1b0753","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nflocculonodular lobe of the cerebellum named for the vestibular input from the eighth cranial nerve","vestibulocerebellum",["vermis","ataxia","spinocerebellum"],"vestibulocerebellum: flocculonodular lobe of the cerebellum named for the vestibular input from the eighth cranial nerve.","The answer begins with V and has 1 word.","fs-id2152546","f-23711967714e"],["q16-7c746601d797","s16.5-6a620a25fa3e","16.5","The Coordination and Gait Exams","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe role of the ____ is a subject of debate.","cerebellum",["vermis","ataxia","gait"],"The role of the cerebellum is a subject of debate.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2250789","f-dee9b61845b3"],["q16-8c59a916c9c8","s16.5-46b0baa6db41","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe cerebellum is located in apposition to the dorsal surface of the ____, centered on the pons.","brain stem",["flocculonodular lobe","neurological exam","check reflex"],"The cerebellum is located in apposition to the dorsal surface of the brain stem, centered on the pons.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2252793","f-61fb7e16fab0"],["q16-7ec62082117e","s16.5-7df96ac8bc32","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the connection of the cerebellum to the midbrain and forebrain.","superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP)",["inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP)","middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP)","flocculonodular lobe"],"The superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP) is the connection of the cerebellum to the midbrain and forebrain.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2252793","f-e906efb4e466"],["q16-d4c4ebf2195a","s16.5-d9f14fc88fa0","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the connection to the medulla.","inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP)",["middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP)","superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP)","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway"],"The inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP) is the connection to the medulla.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2252793","f-f5bba4b680e0"],["q16-7170702e77ea","s16.5-828c047f14f5","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ICP conveys sensory input to the cerebellum, partially from the ____, but also through fibers of the inferior olive.","spinocerebellar tract",["red nucleus","rubrospinal tract","Romberg test"],"The ICP conveys sensory input to the cerebellum, partially from the spinocerebellar tract, but also through fibers of the inferior olive.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2307969","f-54224cd16213"],["q16-a225caecf064","s16.5-99df7bbfcf11","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe MCP is part of the ____ that connects the cerebral cortex with the cerebellum and preferentially targets the lateral regions of the cerebellum.","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway",["coordination exam","neurological exam","flocculonodular lobe"],"The MCP is part of the cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway that connects the cerebral cortex with the cerebellum and preferentially targets the lateral regions of the cerebellum.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2307969","f-89c2abaebf86"],["q16-a923191469c8","s16.5-7a388262398f","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe SCP is the major output of the cerebellum, divided between the ____ in the midbrain and the thalamus, which will return cerebellar processing to the motor cortex.","red nucleus",["inferior olive","procedural memory","coordination exam"],"The SCP is the major output of the cerebellum, divided between the red nucleus in the midbrain and the thalamus, which will return cerebellar processing to the motor cortex.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2307969","f-a6c8b85d2ec8"],["q16-dc577dc2a217","s16.5-ca3c8ef8df8c","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSensory feedback in the form of ____ from the spinal cord, as well as vestibular sensations from the inner ear, enters through the ICP.","proprioception",["vestibulocerebellum","ataxia","cerebrocerebellum"],"Sensory feedback in the form of proprioception from the spinal cord, as well as vestibular sensations from the inner ear, enters through the ICP.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2307969","f-802e61eed597"],["q16-292c293e82bb","s16.5-497979e5c82a","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe red nucleus sends new motor commands to the spinal cord through the ____.","rubrospinal tract",["check reflex","neurological exam","flocculonodular lobe"],"The red nucleus sends new motor commands to the spinal cord through the rubrospinal tract.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2307969","f-baa3aeda629f"],["q16-0027eb9a1f31","s16.5-921cad7aff32","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is referred to as the vestibulocerebellum because of the vestibular projection into that region.","flocculonodular lobe",["rubrospinal tract","red nucleus","neurological exam"],"The flocculonodular lobe is referred to as the vestibulocerebellum because of the vestibular projection into that region.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2152546","f-eb665add1d53"],["q16-b2ba19c923e9","s16.5-99df7bbfcf11","16.5","Location and Connections of the Cerebellum","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe lateral cerebellum is referred to as the cerebrocerebellum, reflecting the significant input from the cerebral cortex through the ____.","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway",["coordination exam","check reflex","red nucleus"],"Finally, the lateral cerebellum is referred to as the cerebrocerebellum, reflecting the significant input from the cerebral cortex through the cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2152546","f-c5a01fc0c4db"],["q16-3b731e3dd3b7","s16.5-d807d3ef18b1","16.5","Coordination and Alternating Movement","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTesting for cerebellar function is the basis of the ____.","coordination exam",["flocculonodular lobe","rubrospinal tract","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway"],"Testing for cerebellar function is the basis of the coordination exam.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2309111","f-0c83225f67b6"],["q16-357e922a5a58","s16.5-bfeeed260fa0","16.5","Coordination and Alternating Movement","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe subtests target appendicular musculature, controlling the limbs, and axial musculature for posture and ____.","gait",["cerebrocerebellum","spinocerebellum","vermis"],"The subtests target appendicular musculature, controlling the limbs, and axial musculature for posture and gait.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2309111","f-d63a54ee78f5"],["q16-216989ae46f3","s16.5-404f42c65aaa","16.5","Coordination and Alternating Movement","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe assessment of cerebellar function will depend on the normal functioning of other systems addressed in previous sections of the ____.","neurological exam",["check reflex","spinocerebellar tract","cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway"],"The assessment of cerebellar function will depend on the normal functioning of other systems addressed in previous sections of the neurological exam.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2309111","f-d5d580946bc4"],["q16-2e71dc4f4503","s16.5-7a388262398f","16.5","Coordination and Alternating Movement","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ would send an additional signal to the LMN for the arm to increase contraction momentarily to overcome the change and regain the original position.","red nucleus",["Romberg test","flocculonodular lobe","neurological exam"],"The red nucleus would send an additional signal to the LMN for the arm to increase contraction momentarily to overcome the change and regain the original position.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1284478","f-1ec0adfc9ac7"],["q16-2fac3f7ad0be","s16.5-fa8aec744675","16.5","Coordination and Alternating Movement","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ depends on cerebellar input to keep increased contraction from continuing after the removal of resistance.","check reflex",["flocculonodular lobe","rubrospinal tract","red nucleus"],"The check reflex depends on cerebellar input to keep increased contraction from continuing after the removal of resistance.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2306534","f-24ff5f4d62cb"],["q16-999e03c4463d","s16.5-181efea957d9","16.5","Coordination and Alternating Movement","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe patient flexes the elbow against ____ from the examiner to extend the elbow.","resistance",["cerebrocerebellum","ataxia","vermis"],"The patient flexes the elbow against resistance from the examiner to extend the elbow.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2306534","f-f0df67553867"],["q16-e0a12c3de14b","s16.5-d807d3ef18b1","16.5","Posture and Gait","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nGait can either be considered a separate part of the neurological exam or a subtest of the ____ that addresses walking and balance.","coordination exam",["cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway","red nucleus","check reflex"],"Gait can either be considered a separate part of the neurological exam or a subtest of the coordination exam that addresses walking and balance.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1547688","f-ca38980672f2"],["q16-2a953e224b9d","s16.5-ecf285d740ab","16.5","Posture and Gait","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThough the station subtest appears to be similar to the ____, the difference is that the patient’s eyes are open during station.","Romberg test",["procedural memory","check reflex","red nucleus"],"Though the station subtest appears to be similar to the Romberg test, the difference is that the patient’s eyes are open during station.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1547688","f-3d3a155dd38d"],["q16-0cbb196f3e50","s16.5-ecf285d740ab","16.5","Posture and Gait","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ has the patient stand still with the eyes closed.","Romberg test",["spinocerebellar tract","red nucleus","flocculonodular lobe"],"The Romberg test has the patient stand still with the eyes closed.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1547688","f-bc3fd8e74910"],["q16-2b55fe4a99a3","s16.5-245ac772a2ca","16.5","Ataxia","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA movement disorder of the cerebellum is referred to as ____.","ataxia",["spinocerebellum","vestibulocerebellum","vermis"],"A movement disorder of the cerebellum is referred to as ataxia.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1339156","f-516e0607bf94"],["q16-4b6e3d215fc7","s16.5-245ac772a2ca","16.5","Ataxia","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is often the result of exposure to exogenous substances, focal lesions, or a genetic disorder.","Ataxia",["cerebrocerebellum","spinocerebellum","gait"],"Ataxia is often the result of exposure to exogenous substances, focal lesions, or a genetic disorder.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2242058","f-079ea245f523"],["q16-aab54e0a1d99","s16.5-6a620a25fa3e","16.5","Ataxia","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFocal lesions include strokes affecting the cerebellar arteries, tumors that may impinge on the ____, trauma to the back of the head and neck, or MS.","cerebellum",["vermis","ataxia","gait"],"Focal lesions include strokes affecting the cerebellar arteries, tumors that may impinge on the cerebellum, trauma to the back of the head and neck, or MS.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2242058","f-d635a676b8fb"],["q16-b4b0b555cd93","s16.5-245ac772a2ca","16.5","Ataxia","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMercury in fish can cause ____ as well.","ataxia",["vermis","spinocerebellum","cerebrocerebellum"],"Mercury in fish can cause ataxia as well.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2242058","f-05f15daaf895"],["q16-9ef1f77a7b52","s16.5-6a620a25fa3e","16.5","The Coordination and Gait Exams","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe examiner would look for issues with balance, which coordinates proprioceptive, vestibular, and visual information in the ____.","cerebellum",["gait","vermis","ataxia"],"The examiner would look for issues with balance, which coordinates proprioceptive, vestibular, and visual information in the cerebellum.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2264756","f-8fec10698346"],["q16-8f4fbc846f95","s16.5-d807d3ef18b1","16.5","The Field Sobriety Test","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA variation of the ____ is the Field Sobriety Test (FST) used to assess whether drivers are under the influence of alcohol.","coordination exam",["neurological exam","Romberg test","flocculonodular lobe"],"A variation of the coordination exam is the Field Sobriety Test (FST) used to assess whether drivers are under the influence of alcohol.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2278336","f-065d5dbb842d"],["q16-7df27024e2cc","s16.5-6a620a25fa3e","16.5","The Field Sobriety Test","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is also very sensitive to ethanol, the particular type of alcohol found in beer, wine, and liquor.","cerebellum",["gait","ataxia","vermis"],"The cerebellum is also very sensitive to ethanol, the particular type of alcohol found in beer, wine, and liquor.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2278336","f-e717209d64c3"],["q16-951d557581c2","s16.5-6a620a25fa3e","16.5","The Field Sobriety Test","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is important because speech production is a coordinated activity.","cerebellum",["vermis","gait","ataxia"],"However, the cerebellum is important because speech production is a coordinated activity.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707176","f-607e607ae568"],["q16-edit-7d472f7afec4","s16.3-edit-94527e8608cc","16.3","The Pupillary Light Response","connections","Which cranial nerves provide the sensory input and parasympathetic motor output of the pupillary light reflex?","Optic nerve and oculomotor nerve",["Olfactory nerve and hypoglossal nerve","Vestibulocochlear nerve and accessory nerve","Facial nerve and abducens nerve"],"The optic nerve carries the light-related sensory input; parasympathetic fibers in the oculomotor nerve help constrict the pupil.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1582811","f-2b03520a571c"]]);
